  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 AM MDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nLow pressure over eastern Oregon will slowly march our way over\nthe next couple of days. Expect another day of occasional \nshowers and thunderstorms. The high resolution models are \nindicating that storms this afternoon could be as intense as \nwhat we experienced on Thursday. With that said, a solid area of\nmarginal risk for severe thunderstorms exists over east Idaho \ntoday with hail and wind as the main threats. Given the heavy \nrain that we saw today, will issue a flood watch for the Big \nRock burn scar near Heise. On Saturday the low will be overhead \nand temperatures will be noticeably colder with highs in the low\nto mid 60s. The cooler temps combined with quite a bit of cloud\ncover around should limit thunderstorm intensity. The low will \nmove east Saturday night into Sunday bringing an end to our rain\nchances. Additional rainfall amounts from 12z Friday through \n06z Sunday range from just a few hundredths across the Magic \nValley to around 0.50 inches across the eastern highlands. Of \ncourse, rainfall amounts are highly dependent on where the \nheavier thunderstorms occur with locally heavy downpours likely \ntoday.\n\nAfter this system passes, looks like we will see several days of dry \nweather with the next low pressure system expected to pass to our \nsouth early next week. Overnight lows continue to look warm enough \nthat widespread frost potential will not be a problem across the \nSnake Plain and Magic Valley. Although some patchy frost could be \npossible Sunday and Monday mornings across the upper Snake Plain.",
